WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.001 --> 00:00:07.352
"The Secret to Weight Loss Through Exercise"

00:00:07.425 --> 00:00:08.640
Most overweight individuals

00:00:08.640 --> 00:00:10.200
evidently tend
to choose exercise

00:00:10.200 --> 00:00:12.120
as their first approach
to weight loss.

00:00:12.120 --> 00:00:15.240
When unrealistic hopes
clash with reality,

00:00:15.240 --> 00:00:17.520
the disappointment
may lead to an abandonment

00:00:17.520 --> 00:00:19.260
of weight-loss efforts
altogether

00:00:19.260 --> 00:00:22.980
as an exercise in futility,
no pun intended.

00:00:23.760 --> 00:00:27.720
Our false expectations may also
give us license to overeat.

00:00:27.720 --> 00:00:30.420
Our pie-in-the-sky notions
about the power of exercise

00:00:30.420 --> 00:00:33.900
may just be used to justify
an extra slice of pie

00:00:33.900 --> 00:00:35.580
right here on earth.

00:00:35.580 --> 00:00:38.220
Some researchers warn
that labeling menus

00:00:38.220 --> 00:00:39.840
with calorie equivalents
of exercise

00:00:39.840 --> 00:00:41.940
could be counterproductive,

00:00:41.940 --> 00:00:45.000
backfiring if people
rationalize their indulgences

00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:45.900
after a workout.

00:00:45.900 --> 00:00:49.560
This concern has actually been
put to the test.

00:00:49.560 --> 00:00:52.440
Exercise psychologists
took a group men and women,

00:00:52.440 --> 00:00:53.940
put them on a stationary bike,

00:00:53.940 --> 00:00:55.680
and had them cycle
until they burned

00:00:55.680 --> 00:00:58.680
either 50 calories
or more than 250 calories.

00:00:58.680 --> 00:01:01.980
Unbeknownst to them,
the researchers

00:01:01.980 --> 00:01:04.560
manipulated the machines
to give false readouts,

00:01:04.560 --> 00:01:06.900
such that in actuality

00:01:06.900 --> 00:01:09.780
both groups burned
the same number of calories.

00:01:09.780 --> 00:01:11.880
They just thought
they burned more or less.

00:01:11.880 --> 00:01:14.220
Then, they were offered a meal
10 minutes later,

00:01:14.220 --> 00:01:15.660
ostensibly to measure

00:01:15.660 --> 00:01:17.820
the "effects of exercise
on taste perception,"

00:01:17.820 --> 00:01:20.760
but the real purpose
was to covertly measure

00:01:20.760 --> 00:01:21.840
how much people ate.

00:01:21.840 --> 00:01:23.640
Those who falsely believed

00:01:23.640 --> 00:01:25.020
they had burned off
more calories

00:01:25.020 --> 00:01:27.780
did seem to demonstrate
a greater "license to eat,"

00:01:27.780 --> 00:01:30.300
ending up eating
significantly more calories,

00:01:30.300 --> 00:01:32.940
mostly in the form
of chocolate chip cookies.

00:01:34.020 --> 00:01:36.240
After a workout,
people may be tempted

00:01:36.240 --> 00:01:38.520
to treat themselves
for their sweaty sacrifice.

00:01:39.180 --> 00:01:40.920
To prevent
this kneejerk reaction

00:01:40.920 --> 00:01:42.240
from undermining our efforts,

00:01:42.240 --> 00:01:44.760
we should strive to make
exercise less of a chore.

00:01:44.760 --> 00:01:48.240
In a paper entitled
"Is it fun or exercise?

00:01:48.240 --> 00:01:51.960
The framing of physical activity
biases subsequent snacking,"

00:01:51.960 --> 00:01:55.680
a study is described in which
individuals were randomized

00:01:55.680 --> 00:01:57.120
to the same amount
of physical activity

00:01:57.120 --> 00:01:58.680
but just described differently.

00:01:59.220 --> 00:02:01.500
Half were told they were going
on a "scenic walk,"

00:02:01.500 --> 00:02:02.880
and the other half were told

00:02:02.880 --> 00:02:04.560
they were going on
an "exercise walk."

00:02:04.560 --> 00:02:07.200
Afterwards,
researchers covertly measured

00:02:07.200 --> 00:02:09.660
how much dessert everyone took
at a subsequent meal.

00:02:09.660 --> 00:02:12.180
Those in the
movement-as-exercise group

00:02:12.180 --> 00:02:13.500
reportedly served themselves

00:02:13.500 --> 00:02:16.020
about 35 percent more
chocolate pudding

00:02:16.020 --> 00:02:17.760
than the movement-as-fun group.

00:02:17.760 --> 00:02:20.400
This is all the more reason
to choose activities

00:02:20.400 --> 00:02:21.360
that are enjoyable,

00:02:21.360 --> 00:02:24.000
such as walking with friends
or while listening to music,

00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:26.220
or watching a video
on the treadmill.

00:02:26.220 --> 00:02:29.700
Reframing exercise as play
rather than work

00:02:29.700 --> 00:02:32.520
may not only make
for a more sustainable regimen

00:02:32.520 --> 00:02:34.380
but may make us less likely

00:02:34.380 --> 00:02:36.780
to consciously
or unconsciously feel the need

00:02:36.780 --> 00:02:39.480
to later reward ourselves
at the buffet line.

00:02:40.380 --> 00:02:42.600
Even just thinking
about exercise

00:02:42.600 --> 00:02:44.700
may compel people
to eat more food.

00:02:44.700 --> 00:02:47.880
Those randomized to simply read
about physical activity

00:02:47.880 --> 00:02:50.940
went on to serve themselves
nearly 60 percent more M&amp;Ms

00:02:50.940 --> 00:02:52.080
than those
in the control group,

00:02:52.080 --> 00:02:54.180
adding up to hundreds
of extra calories.

00:02:54.180 --> 00:02:57.780
The researchers concluded:
"simply imagining exercise

00:02:57.780 --> 00:03:00.960
leads participants to serve
themselves more food."

00:03:02.040 --> 00:03:03.600
Expending energy
through exercise

00:03:03.600 --> 00:03:06.360
may not just psychologically
predispose us to eat more

00:03:06.360 --> 00:03:09.060
but may physiologically
make us hungrier.

00:03:09.660 --> 00:03:12.480
We evolved in the context
of scarcity,

00:03:12.480 --> 00:03:14.760
so our body
places great value

00:03:14.760 --> 00:03:17.400
on rapidly replenishing
lost fat stores.

00:03:17.400 --> 00:03:20.220
This helps explain why
the average weight loss

00:03:20.220 --> 00:03:21.240
with exercise training

00:03:21.240 --> 00:03:23.460
is only 30 percent
of that predicted

00:03:23.460 --> 00:03:25.500
based on the number
of extra calories burned.

00:03:25.500 --> 00:03:27.900
Calories in
versus calories out

00:03:27.900 --> 00:03:29.340
can be complicated
by the fact

00:03:29.340 --> 00:03:32.602
that changes on one side
of the equation can affect the other side.

00:03:32.602 --> 00:03:35.574
In other words,
we can work up an appetite.

00:03:35.574 --> 00:03:36.960
Carefully controlled studies

00:03:36.960 --> 00:03:39.240
show that caloric intake
tends to rise over time

00:03:39.240 --> 00:03:41.820
to match any increase
in caloric expenditure,

00:03:41.820 --> 00:03:44.460
making significant weight loss
through exercise alone

00:03:44.460 --> 00:03:45.960
remarkably difficult.

00:03:45.960 --> 00:03:48.720
This doesn't happen
over a day or two.

00:03:48.720 --> 00:03:49.560
After a workout,

00:03:49.560 --> 00:03:52.440
there may not be
an immediate increase in hunger,

00:03:52.440 --> 00:03:54.780
but averaged over the week
or weeks,

00:03:54.780 --> 00:03:58.320
our appetite does tend
to increase to balance out

00:03:58.320 --> 00:04:00.720
most of the extra calories
we've been burning.

00:04:00.720 --> 00:04:04.560
This calorie compensation
isn't perfect, though,

00:04:04.560 --> 00:04:07.020
so we can end up
with a net loss in body fat,

00:04:07.020 --> 00:04:09.300
particularly at
higher exercise levels.

00:04:09.300 --> 00:04:11.640
So, the secret to weight loss
through exercise

00:04:11.640 --> 00:04:13.140
may be sheer volume,

00:04:13.140 --> 00:04:14.820
at least 300 minutes a week

00:04:14.820 --> 00:04:17.160
to achieve
appreciable fat loss.

00:04:18.120 --> 00:04:20.520
This regulation of our appetite
through activity

00:04:20.520 --> 00:04:21.660
works in both directions.

00:04:21.660 --> 00:04:24.360
Just as there exists
a higher level of exercise

00:04:24.360 --> 00:04:27.240
where we can start to outpace
our appetite and lose weight,

00:04:27.240 --> 00:04:29.580
there's a lower level
of exercise

00:04:29.580 --> 00:04:31.320
where our body
loses the ability

00:04:31.320 --> 00:04:33.840
to sufficiently downgrade
our appetite,

00:04:33.840 --> 00:04:34.980
and we gain weight.

00:04:35.520 --> 00:04:37.980
This sedentary zone
where our appetite

00:04:37.980 --> 00:04:39.660
becomes uncoupled
from our activity level

00:04:39.660 --> 00:04:43.080
appears to start
at around 7,100 steps a day.

00:04:43.860 --> 00:04:46.620
So, let's say you start out
as a really active person,

00:04:46.620 --> 00:04:49.440
chowing down on
nearly 2,900 calories a day,

00:04:49.440 --> 00:04:52.140
and, for whatever reason,
have to cut back on exercise.

00:04:52.140 --> 00:04:54.480
You'd think you'd gain
a lot of weight,

00:04:54.480 --> 00:04:56.700
but you're surprised
that you don't.

00:04:56.700 --> 00:05:00.600
Basically, no increased odds
of gaining significant body fat.

00:05:00.600 --> 00:05:02.160
What happened?

00:05:02.160 --> 00:05:06.180
With your drop in exercise came
an inadvertent drop in appetite.

00:05:06.180 --> 00:05:09.240
But there's a limit to how far
our appetite can drop.

00:05:09.240 --> 00:05:10.680
Once you cross that threshold,

00:05:10.680 --> 00:05:14.040
once you dip below logging
at least 7,100 steps or so

00:05:14.040 --> 00:05:15.480
a day on your pedometer,

00:05:15.480 --> 00:05:18.780
your appetite doesn't slow
much further to match,

00:05:18.780 --> 00:05:21.120
and the pounds can start
to pile on.

00:05:21.120 --> 00:05:24.000
Your body tries
to keep your weight steady

00:05:24.000 --> 00:05:25.140
by adjusting your appetite,

00:05:25.140 --> 00:05:26.520
but we just weren't designed

00:05:26.520 --> 00:05:29.160
to handle such extreme
low levels of movement

00:05:29.160 --> 00:05:34.657
that sadly characterizes
most of the U.S. population.

